From d49a4cb5d52e64007da546467ddd5fb8ddb08915 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:16:35 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] widgets on the GPU: the pair where the EGL has no surface
 with two views

A window painted once per eye on the GPU was shown flat on an EGL without EGL_EXT_multiview_window (NVIDIA's
own driver), and that was said. Now the Wayland EGL window makes its one buffer twice as wide when the
widgets' repaint manager composes a pair itself (_q_stereo_pair with _q_stereo_views 2), declares it as the
shared memory store declares its pair (full side by side, the desktop's own depth, QWaylandStereoDepth::setPair,
now exported) and says so with _q_stereo_side_by_side_buffer; the OpenGL swapchain takes that width, and the
backing store compositor draws the pair texture whole and each render-to-texture child into both halves.
The change happens when the wish changes, on the GUI thread, before the frame that draws the pair.
A window with a frame the client draws stays flat, as on every path.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2 via Claude Code

A pair that is still shown on a surface with one view (no compositor support for the declaration) is told
through QT_WIDGETS_GPU_PAINT_TELL as flat-pair.
